# YouTube video downloader

This is a small web downloader for MP4 video and MP3 audio. It runs in a
Docker container and lets a computer or phone download the result through a
browser.

## Requirements

- Docker Desktop on Windows, or Docker Engine on Linux
- A running Docker daemon
- Permission to download the requested content

## Build the image

From the repository directory:

```powershell
docker build -t youtube-downloader .
```

The image includes .NET, `yt-dlp`, FFmpeg, and Deno. Deno is needed by
`yt-dlp` for some YouTube extraction paths.

## Run locally

On Windows PowerShell:

```powershell
New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force .\downloads | Out-Null
docker run --rm -p 8080:8080 -v "${PWD}\downloads:/downloads" youtube-downloader
```

On Linux:

```bash
mkdir -p downloads
docker run --rm -p 8080:8080 -v "$(pwd)/downloads:/downloads" youtube-downloader
```

Open `http://localhost:8080` in your browser. Paste a YouTube URL, choose MP3
or MP4, and the browser will start downloading the file to the computer or
phone.

## Run on a server

Copy this repository to the server, install Docker, start the Docker daemon,
and run the build command from the repository directory. Then run:

```bash
mkdir -p downloads
docker run -d --name youtube-downloader \
  --restart unless-stopped \
  -p 8080:8080 \
  -v "$(pwd)/downloads:/downloads" \
  youtube-downloader
```

On the same server, use `http://localhost:8080`. From another device on the
same network, use `http://SERVER-IP:8080`. For internet access, put the
service behind HTTPS and authentication rather than exposing port 8080
directly.

The server keeps each temporary download only until it has been sent to the
browser. The mounted `downloads` directory is still available for server-side
inspection and future configuration.

## Stop the server

```bash
docker stop youtube-downloader
docker rm youtube-downloader
```
